    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. could you dive a bit deeper into the actual mechanism of how leucine is \"sensed\" by the cell? like, is it through the Sestrin2 protein or something else? i want to be able to explain the cellular level stuff to him clearly.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Leucine is sensed primarily by its ability to inhibit the negative regulator, GATOR2, which subsequently allows the activation of the Rag GTPases. The Rag GTPases then correctly position mTORC1 on the lysosomal surface where necessary activators reside. While Sestrin proteins are known to inhibit mTOR when energy or amino acid levels are low, leucine's presence overrides this inhibition through this direct upstream activation mechanism. This swift signaling allows the cell to quickly recognize the presence of abundant amino acid fuel.",
      "source": "target",
