    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so the lysosomal surface is basically the command center for this. how does the cell ensure this process doesn't go into overdrive? like, are there specific feedback loops that prevent excessive hypertrophy or metabolic waste when leucine levels are consistently high?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The cell employs complex negative feedback loops to prevent mTORC1 from operating uncontrollably. Once mTORC1 is highly active, it can phosphorylate downstream targets, some of which then feedback to dampen its own signaling, acting as a natural brake. Furthermore, chronic over-stimulation can lead to changes in cellular metabolism and the expression of regulatory proteins, putting the system into a state of homeostatic balance. These mechanisms ensure that muscle growth is adaptive and proportional to the body's current energy and nutrient availability.",
      "source": "target",
